# toolbox — the domain a toybox applet lands in when init execs it.
#
# AOSP's public/toolbox.te is eight rules long and its comment says the only
# known usage is mkswap via fs_mgr. On this device it has a second user, and
# that user is a thermal mitigation.
#
# WHO IS IN THIS DOMAIN HERE. rootdir/init.m95.cpuset.rc:20 starts
#   service m95_cpuset /system/bin/sh /m95-cpuset.sh
# with no `seclabel`, so the shell itself stays in init's domain; the `cat`
# processes it forks are toybox, hit toolbox_exec, and domain_auto_trans into
# toolbox. Both denials below are those `cat`s.
#
# WHAT THE SCRIPT DOES (rootdir/m95-cpuset.sh, and its header carries the
# measurements): this kernel has no cpuset_v2_mode, so cgroup v1 drops a cpu
# from cpus_allowed when hotplug parks it and never restores it. /dev/cpuset/cpus
# decays monotonically until every cpuset on this deca-core holds a single A53 -
# measured: online was 0-2,4-9 while the root cpuset was down to a bare 4, load
# average 20, and the phone ran HOTTER because the same work took far longer.
# The script re-reads the online mask every 5 s and rewrites the root set and
# its children.

##########################
# FACT (live device, 2026-08-10):
#   avc: denied { read } for comm="cat" name="cpus" dev="cgroup" ino=8
#        scontext=u:r:toolbox:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:cgroup:s0
#        tclass=file  permissive=1                                       (x3)
#
# FACT: `ls -lZ /dev/cpuset/cpus` -> u:object_r:cgroup:s0, and /proc/mounts
# shows /dev/cpuset is a cgroup mount (cpuset,noprefix,release_agent=...).
#
# Read, specifically. system/sepolicy/public/domain.te:245-246 already gives
# EVERY domain cgroup:dir { search write } and cgroup:file w_file_perms, and
# w_file_perms is { open append write lock map } - which is why the audit shows
# only { read } and not { open }: the open was already allowed and the read was
# not. The platform hands out write on these files freely and withholds read.
#
# WHAT BREAKS WITHOUT IT, under enforcing: `root_cpus=$(cat /dev/cpuset/cpus)`
# comes back empty, `[ -n "$root_cpus" ] || return 0` bails, and sync_once does
# nothing. The 5 s loop keeps spinning, exits nothing, logs nothing and stops
# mitigating - so the cpusets collapse back to one core exactly as measured
# above, with no denial left in the log to say why. This is the loudest example
# on the device of a mitigation that keeps running after it stops working.
#
# Narrow by DOMAIN rather than by type, deliberately. A tighter target type is
# not reachable: cgroup labels come from `genfscon cgroup /`, and every cgroup
# hierarchy on this device (/acct, /dev/cpuctl, /dev/cpuset) is the same
# filesystem TYPE, so a genfscon path prefix cannot distinguish the cpuset mount
# from the others. toolbox is the smallest domain in the policy and only init
# may enter it (`neverallow { domain -init } toolbox:process transition`), so
# read on cgroup files here is a much smaller grant than the write the platform
# already gives all 300-odd domains.
#
# `getattr` is included on inference, not observation: only { read } was audited,
# but getattr is the standard companion of a read on a proc/sysfs-like file and
# a second denial on it would reproduce the same silent failure. Nothing else
# is granted - note in particular that public/domain.te:1407 neverallows
# cgroup:file create for every domain, so this must stay a read.
allow toolbox cgroup:file { read getattr };

##########################
# FACT (live device, 2026-08-10):
#   avc: denied { write } for comm="cat" path="pipe:[N]"
#        scontext=u:r:toolbox:s0 tcontext=u:r:init:s0
#        tclass=fifo_file  permissive=1                                  (x3)
#
# The other half of the same defect, and the half that breaks BOTH reads in the
# script rather than one. `online=$(cat ...)` and `root_cpus=$(cat ...)` are
# command substitutions: the shell (init domain) creates the pipe, so the pipe
# inode carries init's label, and the `cat` (toolbox domain) writing into it is
# a cross-domain fifo_file write. public/domain.te:28 grants
# `allow domain self:{ fifo_file file } rw_file_perms` - self only, which does
# not cover an inherited pipe created by a different domain.
#
# WHAT BREAKS WITHOUT IT, under enforcing: both command substitutions return
# empty. `[ -n "$online" ]` is false so the root cpuset is never rewritten, and
# `[ -n "$root_cpus" ]` is false so the children are never rewritten either.
# Note the failure is asymmetric and that is what makes it silent: the
# `echo "$root_cpus" > /dev/cpuset/.../cpus` redirects are shell BUILTINS
# running in the init domain and would still succeed - the script would keep
# writing, it would just have nothing to write.
#
# Scoped to init because init is the only domain that may exec into toolbox, so
# it is the only domain whose pipes a toolbox process can ever inherit. `write`
# only - not read, not getattr, not ioctl; a writer end of a substitution pipe
# needs nothing else.
#
# FOLLOW-UP worth taking, which would delete both rules above: rewrite the two
# command substitutions in m95-cpuset.sh as mksh input redirections
# (`read online < /sys/devices/system/cpu/online`). That keeps the read in the
# shell, so no toolbox process and no pipe is created at all. It was not done
# here because it moves the cgroup read into the INIT domain, and this tree's
# init.te opens with an explicit instruction not to add power to init - a rule
# on the eight-line toolbox domain is the cheaper of the two.
allow toolbox init:fifo_file write;
